@Tutorial(time: 35) {
    @Intro(title: "Character animation") {
        In this final lesson, we'll bring our characters to life using a
        mix of glTF-embedded animations, a loaded animation resource,
        and a hand-authored animation.

        The characters are already partly animated. The player switches between
        the axolotl's built-in `Idle` and `Run` cycles — we wired that up back
        when we wrote the movement code — and the snake's `Move` animation
        autoplays from its wrapper scene.

        What's missing is the custom work. In this lesson we'll add a second
        `AnimationPlayer` to each gameplay scene: one holding a jump arc for
        the player (loaded from a ready-made resource) and one holding a sway
        for the mobs (keyed by hand in the editor). Then we'll drive both from
        code.
    }
    @Section(title: "Adding the jump animation to the player") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Let's start with the player. We'll add a scene-level
            `AnimationPlayer` and load `jump.res` into it.

            @Image(source: 10-03.jump-animation-setup.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the 3D view, open `player.tscn`. Select the `Player` node and add a
                child
                [AnimationPlayer](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fanimationplayer.html#class-animationplayer).

                @Image(source: 10-04.add-animation-player-player.png, alt: "Add an AnimationPlayer child to the Player node")
            }
            @Step {
                With the `AnimationPlayer` selected, head to the
                _Animation Drawer_ at the bottom of the screen. You can open it by tapping **Animation**.
                
                Tap **Options** and then **Edit Animations**. 
            }
            @Step {
                In the new window, tap **New Library** to create a new
                `AnimationLibrary` sub-resource. You can name it anything.

                Expand it by taping on it and tap **Load Animation into Library**
                then navigate to `resources/jump.res` and tap **Select**.

                @Image(source: 10-05.load-jump-resource.png, alt: "Load jump.res into the AnimationLibrary")
            }
            You can close the window.

            Now we'll update the player script in three small steps.

            @Step {
                Open the `player.gd` script and add an `@onready` reference to
                the scene `AnimationPlayer` we just created.

                The reference to the model's own `AnimationPlayer` is already
                there — we added it when we taught the character to run.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-3d-11a.gd, previousFile: player-3d-10.gd)
            }

            Next we'll improve how the jump looks.

            @Step {
                Tilt the `Pivot` based on the character's vertical velocity to
                give the jump a nice arc — nose up on the way up, nose down on
                the way back down.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-3d-11b.gd, previousFile: player-3d-11a.gd)
            }

            Finally, we'll update the `bounce()` function to play the custom jump
            animation when bouncing off mobs.

            @Step {
                Update the `bounce()` function to play the custom jump animation
                from the scene `AnimationPlayer`, so squashing a mob looks the
                same as a deliberate jump.

                The skeleton looks after itself here: the animation block in
                `_physics_process()` already switches to `Idle` the moment the
                bounce lifts the character off the floor.

                > Note: When playing an animation, we use the full library path
                > `"Animations/jump.res"` rather than just the filename. Godot's
                > `AnimationPlayer` requires the library name followed by the
                > animation name when referencing animations.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-3d-11.gd, previousFile: player-3d-11b.gd)
            }

            @Step {
                Here is the complete `player.gd` so you can check your code
                against it.
                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-3d-11.gd, reset: true)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Animating the mobs") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The mobs get the same treatment. The snake's `Move`
            animation is already autoplaying from the wrapper scene. Now
            we'll **author** a gentle looping sway by hand so we can learn how that is done
            and add it to a scene-level `AnimationPlayer`.
            Then we'll scale both players' speed with the mob's random
            movement speed, so faster mobs also animate faster.

            Authoring the sway ourselves is a great introduction to the
            animation editor. The sway itself is simple: three
            keyframes that slide the `Pivot` a little to the side and
            back, looping forever. We'll use round numbers for both the
            timing (`0`, `0.6`, and `1.2` seconds) and the slide
            distance (`0.5` units), so it's easy to follow and to tweak
            later.

            @Image(source: 10-06.mob-animation-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open `mob.tscn`. Select the `Mob` node and add a child
                [AnimationPlayer](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fanimationplayer.html#class-animationplayer).

                @Image(source: 10-07.add-animation-player-mob.png, alt: "Add an AnimationPlayer child to the Mob node")
            }
            @Step {
                With the `AnimationPlayer` selected, open the
                _Animation_ panel at the bottom of the editor. Tap the
                _Animation_ button and choose _New Animation_. Name it
                `Sway`.

                In the animation's settings at the top of the panel,
                turn on the **Loop** toggle so the sway plays
                continuously, and set the animation length to `1.2`
                seconds.

                @Image(source: 10-08.new-sway-animation.png, alt: "Create a new animation named Sway, enable looping, set length to 1.2 seconds")
            }
            @Step {
                Now we key the sway. Select the `Pivot` node. Leave the
                playhead at `0.0` seconds, so the `Pivot` is at its
                rest position. In the _Inspector_, hover the _Position_
                property and tap the **key** icon that appears next to
                it — this inserts a keyframe for the `Pivot`'s position
                at time `0.0`.

                @Image(source: 10-09.key-pivot-position-0.png, alt: "Key the Pivot's position at time 0.0")
            }
            @Step {
                Move the playhead to `0.6` seconds. In the _Inspector_,
                change the `Pivot`'s _Position_ **X** value by `0.5` —
                either add or subtract `0.5`, the direction doesn't
                matter, you just want a small sideways slide. Tap the
                key icon next to _Position_ to insert the second
                keyframe.

                You should see the `Pivot` (and the snake) jump to the
                side in the viewport.

                > Tip: You want the slide to go *across* the snake's
                > body, not along its length. Depending on how you
                > oriented the `Pivot` when you built the mob scene,
                > that may be the X axis or the Z axis. If sliding X
                > moves the snake forward or back instead of
                > side-to-side, undo and slide Z by `0.5` instead.

                @Image(source: 10-10.key-pivot-slide-0-6.png, alt: "At 0.6s, slide the Pivot 0.5 units on X and key it")
            }
            @Step {
                Move the playhead to `1.2` seconds. Set the `Pivot`'s
                _Position_ back to the value you keyed at `0.0` (the
                start position). Tap the key icon to insert the third
                keyframe.

                Press the _Play_ button in the animation panel to
                preview the sway. The snake should slide to the side
                and back, then repeat.

                > Note: We used a `0.5` unit slide over `1.2` seconds
                > because the numbers are easy to read and the result
                > is a gentle, visible lean. Once it works, try
                > doubling the slide to `1.0` unit or halving the
                > length to `0.6` seconds to see how the feel changes.

                @Image(source: 10-11.key-pivot-return-1-2.png, alt: "At 1.2s, return the Pivot to its start position and key it")
            }
            @Step {
                Finally, set the `AnimationPlayer`'s _Autoplay_
                property (in the _Inspector_) to `Sway` so the mob
                starts swaying as soon as it spawns.

                @Image(source: 10-12.autoplay-sway-animation.png, alt: "Set the AnimationPlayer autoplay to Sway")
            }
            @Step {
                Open `mob.gd`. We add `@onready` references to both
                animation players, a `min_anim_speed`/`max_anim_speed`
                range, and a `_sync_animation_speed()` helper that maps
                the mob's movement speed onto that range. We call it at
                the end of `initialize()`.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-07.gd, previousFile: mob-3d-06.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                Here is the complete `mob.gd` for reference.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-07.gd, reset: true)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Trying it out") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            That's the whole game. Run the project on an iPhone or iPad and
            the on-screen joystick appears wherever you touch the left half
            of the screen. Tap and drag to move the axolotl: it switches
            between `Idle` and `Run` as you go, tilts through the arc of a
            jump, and the snakes sway as they cross the platform.

            Land on a snake to squash it and you'll see the custom jump
            animation play on the bounce. The rest of the input — jump, and
            tapping to retry — already works through the on-screen controls,
            an external keyboard, or a gamepad.
        }
        @Steps {
        }
    }
}
